1
00:00:00,106 --> 00:00:03,311
An AI writes a fix for your C++ bug.

2
00:00:03,311 --> 00:00:06,077
The diff is correct. The logic is right.

3
00:00:06,077 --> 00:00:08,763
And then the patch tool says: failed.

4
00:00:08,763 --> 00:00:11,941
The model never finds out why. So it guesses.

5
00:00:11,941 --> 00:00:13,704
And you pay for another round.

6
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:19,625
This is the real bottleneck of agentic coding, and it is not the model.

7
00:00:19,625 --> 00:00:24,758
A model's diff drifts. The line numbers are off by two, because the file changed

8
00:00:24,758 --> 00:00:27,950
underneath it. The context is incomplete.

9
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:32,471
The whitespace does not match. The patch tool hard-fails on all three.

10
00:00:32,471 --> 00:00:35,048
And all it gives you back is an exit code.

11
00:00:36,808 --> 00:00:42,539
FixIt is a C++20 library and command-line tool that closes that circle.

12
00:00:42,539 --> 00:00:47,167
Compile. Locate. Patch. Apply. Re-verify.

13
00:00:47,167 --> 00:00:50,371
It is built for the patch a model actually produces.

14
00:00:50,371 --> 00:00:54,959
And when a patch cannot be applied, it returns an explanation the model can act

15
00:00:54,959 --> 00:00:56,952
on. Not an exit code.

16
00:00:58,858 --> 00:01:05,241
Stage one. It runs the real compiler, clang or GCC, and parses diagnostics into

17
00:01:05,241 --> 00:01:11,412
data, not text. Stage two. Each diagnostic is mapped to the function that encloses

18
00:01:11,412 --> 00:01:14,808
it, with a numbered snippet the model can quote back.

19
00:01:16,428 --> 00:01:19,486
Stage three. The model proposes a diff.

20
00:01:19,486 --> 00:01:24,101
Yours, over https, or the built-in offline mock.

21
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:26,454
Stage four is the heart of FixIt.

22
00:01:26,454 --> 00:01:29,553
Every plausible position in the file is scored.

23
00:01:29,553 --> 00:01:33,103
Exact lines, fuzzy lines, mismatches.

24
00:01:33,103 --> 00:01:36,162
A perfect hunk scores one point zero zero.

25
00:01:36,162 --> 00:01:42,944
Then it searches outward. Zero, one, two, five, ten, fifty lines of drift.

26
00:01:42,944 --> 00:01:48,462
And finally, the whole file. A hunk applies when the score clears zero point eight,

27
00:01:48,462 --> 00:01:51,109
and at least one line matches exactly.

28
00:01:51,109 --> 00:01:54,952
Line drift, missing context, trailing whitespace.

29
00:01:54,952 --> 00:01:56,580
All of it applies anyway.

30
00:01:58,486 --> 00:02:04,816
Stage five. It recompiles. A model that announces it is done still triggers a compile.

31
00:02:04,816 --> 00:02:07,428
Only a clean exit code ends the loop.

32
00:02:09,034 --> 00:02:11,242
And here is the part nothing else gives you.

33
00:02:11,242 --> 00:02:15,005
When a hunk is refused, FixIt says exactly why.

34
00:02:15,005 --> 00:02:19,899
The score. The gate it missed. The line it was declared at, and the line it actually

35
00:02:19,899 --> 00:02:23,077
matched. The exact text that differed.

36
00:02:23,077 --> 00:02:26,574
And a suggested re-read. That is not an error message.

37
00:02:26,574 --> 00:02:29,184
That is the next prompt, ready to paste.

38
00:02:31,691 --> 00:02:35,321
This is a recorded run, and it is byte-reproducible.

39
00:02:35,321 --> 00:02:39,151
The repository ships the transcript, and CI diffs it.

40
00:02:39,151 --> 00:02:42,821
Five compiler errors, repaired in two iterations.

41
00:02:42,821 --> 00:02:47,980
The first hunk lands exactly. The second is declared one line off, and the window

42
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:53,100
search finds it anyway. The second example carries the same bugs fifty lines further

43
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:56,212
down, so both declared positions are wrong.

44
00:02:56,212 --> 00:02:58,645
It still repairs. And the third?

45
00:02:58,645 --> 00:03:01,318
That one is outside the mock's rule set.

46
00:03:01,318 --> 00:03:04,589
FixIt reports it unfixed, and exits one.

47
00:03:04,589 --> 00:03:09,230
It does not pretend. Across the measured sweep, one hundred percent of patches

48
00:03:09,230 --> 00:03:11,784
apply up to twenty lines of drift.

49
00:03:11,784 --> 00:03:15,095
Ninety-nine percent at fifty. The honest limit.

50
00:03:15,095 --> 00:03:20,055
When every block in the file is byte-identical and the line number is wrong, no

51
00:03:20,055 --> 00:03:22,475
algorithm can recover the intent.

52
00:03:22,475 --> 00:03:25,992
That cell collapses. The repository says so.

53
00:03:27,752 --> 00:03:30,265
FixIt is not a wrapper around patch.

54
00:03:30,265 --> 00:03:33,337
Everything below the agent is a plain library.

55
00:03:33,337 --> 00:03:36,209
Link libfixit and drive the loop yourself.

56
00:03:36,209 --> 00:03:39,972
A CI repair bot can take the compiler and the patch engine.

57
00:03:39,972 --> 00:03:42,991
An IDE plugin can take just the code map.

58
00:03:42,991 --> 00:03:45,411
An agent product can take the whole thing.

59
00:03:45,411 --> 00:03:49,456
Four dependencies. No globals. MIT licensed.

60
00:03:51,089 --> 00:03:53,988
Three commands. Nothing touches the network.

61
00:03:53,988 --> 00:03:57,206
The default model is offline and deterministic.

62
00:03:57,206 --> 00:04:00,820
Compile. Locate. Patch. Verify.

